  "notes": "The defense takes the first 2 spade tricks and switches to diamonds. South has at least 1 certain loser in clubs and, therefore, cannot afford to misguess diamonds.\n\nIt is necessary to assume that West has the ♣A. If East holds that card, there will be 2 club losers and the contract will have no chance.\n\nOnce the assumption about the ♣A is made, the position of the diamond honours can be inferred. If West had the ♦A in addition to the ♠A and ♠K already shown, and the ♣A we are assuming he has, he would have 15 points, enough to open 1NT. If West's hand was unbalanced, preventing him from opening 1NT, he surely would have competed more vigorously in the bidding.\n\nIf we assume that West has the ♣A, it is almost a sure thing that East has the ♦A. South should, therefore, play the ♦J from dummy.\n\nWhen South regains the lead, he can draw trump and lead clubs twice towards the dummy.",
  "commentary_paragraphs": [
